#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 540.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125584} :activity/id #uuid "00abd2c0-69a8-5936-a61c-3bf473fe1def" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048515 :topic/id #uuid "a5c178bb-5c36-5147-b26e-1a8bcca7e43c" :topic/math-academy-id 1905} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125331 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125756 :tutorial/content "For any non-constant polynomial $P(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to\\pm\\infty}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{P(x)}\\right)  = 0.\n$$\n\nThis is because as $x$ increases, any polynomial $P(x)$ will grow without bound, and therefore its reciprocal shrinks to zero.\n\nFor example, the expression $x^3 - x^2 + 1$ is a non-constant polynomial, so we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to\\pm\\infty}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{x^3 - x^2 + 1}\\right)  = 0.\n$$\n\nLikewise, the expression $(2x-5)^4$ can be multiplied out to become a non-constant polynomial, so we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to\\pm\\infty}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{ (2x-5)^4 }\\right)  = 0.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "53993a40-4ef1-5068-ac68-7d738ba2a963" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3125 :tutorial/title "Limits at Infinity of Reciprocals of Polynomials"} :step/id #uuid "744f7f66-3061-568b-9ca1-83bca1d8c218" :step/math-academy-id 17038 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125495}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125493 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124573 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124386 :question/id #uuid "ca99dd0a-e2bc-5b6a-8138-ffaac453a0d5" :question/math-academy-id "e-773"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d13b888-0ef7-5194-a54d-435d9b344749" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049313} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050259}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125174} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125199} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233858} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234066} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565411} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565484} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565485}] :knowledge-point/title "Limits at Infinity of Reciprocal Functions"} :step/id #uuid "f653ed64-212d-5ba6-9f81-2b68d9576679" :step/math-academy-id 2644 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125331}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125495 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124575 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124387 :question/id #uuid "96ecee6e-f551-5ecd-9991-0f962190f382" :question/math-academy-id "e-774"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b50ced1a-50ab-57f3-a60d-dd1d9d5bd997" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124874} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125200} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233859} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233860} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565412} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565444}] :knowledge-point/title "Limits at Infinity of Reciprocals of Polynomials"} :step/id #uuid "e035b05c-e5d1-5bb1-9218-53af6ba978ba" :step/math-academy-id 2645 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125585}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125584 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125719 :tutorial/content "How do we calculate $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to\\infty}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right)$ and $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to-\\infty}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right)?$ To find out, let's look at the graph of $y=\\dfrac{1}{x}.$\n\n![](images/ea/ea736c37f855415e54d8b3a3775fab07159b5de4a368bee45bba13ae261372da.png)\n\nWe see that the curve approaches zero as $x$ approaches positive infinity. The same is true as $x$ approaches negative infinity. So, we write \n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right)=0 \\qquad \\textrm{and} \\qquad \\lim_\\limits{x \\to -\\infty} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right)=0.\n$$\n\nThe same is true for $\\dfrac{1}{x^n}$ where $n$ is *any* positive integer. For example,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})=0\\, & \\text{and}\\,{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})=0 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})=0\\, & \\text{and}\\,{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})=0 \\\\  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand so on. This is because as the size of $x$ increases, the size of $\\dfrac{1}{x^n}$ decreases." :tutorial/id #uuid "cc60ef45-0775-563f-b940-b75c2072bb10" :tutorial/math-academy-id 198 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "18483bb1-f2e9-5d0c-9086-c24211966036" :step/math-academy-id 366 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125493}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125585 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125720 :tutorial/content "Let's again look at the graph of $y=\\dfrac{1}{x}.$\n\n![](images/e8/e8b24529b70e32a32eb45fc9ce5d907ca74020a940b46fbf63b5386bf2d8cd9d.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 0^-} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right) = -\\infty \\qquad \\textrm{and} \\qquad \\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 0^+} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right) = \\infty.\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are not the same, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 0} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right) = \\textrm{DNE}.\n$$\n\nAny function of the form $y = \\dfrac{1}{x^n},$ where $n$ is an *odd* natural number, has the same shape as $y = \\dfrac{1}{x},$ and consequently the limit behavior is the same.  For example,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})=-\\infty , & \\,{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})=\\infty , \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}})=-\\infty , & \\,{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}})=\\infty , \\\\  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand so on. For all of the above, the limit of the function does not exist at $x=0$ because the left and right limits are not the same." :tutorial/id #uuid "c947a280-46a8-5dc9-ba95-a6a2486bc4e7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 200 :tutorial/title "Infinite Limits of Reciprocal Functions Where the Leading Term Has an Odd Power"} :step/id #uuid "c7a8cf4a-7017-5fdf-b283-ad717892866d" :step/math-academy-id 369 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125586}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125586 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124626 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124286 :question/id #uuid "1fd5312f-c992-5218-bcfd-9bc235e9cd53" :question/math-academy-id "e-41"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f015a46e-7493-558d-a1d8-c2d90f9306ab" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051049}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124986} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125145} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233861} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234509} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234510} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565415} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565476}] :knowledge-point/title "Infinite Limits of Reciprocal Functions Where the Leading Term Has an Odd Power"} :step/id #uuid "b51ac528-c3e2-5c7f-b774-1a784b67608a" :step/math-academy-id 370 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125587}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125587 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125721 :tutorial/content "Now let's look at the graph of $y = \\dfrac{1}{x^2}.$\n\n![](images/b7/b75253dcc54e0c833995611513b17cb8563249cb5aeb32078508d2c43556313b.png)\n\nWe see that as we approach $0$ from both sides, the function grows rapidly without bound. So \n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 0^-}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{x^2}\\right) = \\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 0^+} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x^2}\\right)= \\infty,\n$$\n\nwhich implies that\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 0} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x^2}\\right)= \\infty.\n$$\n\nAny function of the form $y = \\dfrac{1}{x^n}$ (where $n$ is an *even* natural number) has the same shape as $y = \\dfrac{1}{x^2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 0} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x^n}\\right)= \\infty, \\qquad n=2,4,6,\\ldots\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "298d4a07-7a45-5ab1-9d41-8d9609e000c2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 202 :tutorial/title "Infinite Limits of Reciprocal Functions Where the Leading Term Has an Even Power"} :step/id #uuid "fca491c8-71f7-5bef-9cd1-85fad2457174" :step/math-academy-id 372 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125589}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125589 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124627 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124289 :question/id #uuid "08d460c2-32e7-5e20-86cf-4a1c2bf13c28" :question/math-academy-id "e-43"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "038fb60a-e596-5e48-b05e-f8414d454518" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124952} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125160} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233862} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234239} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234511} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565409} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565422} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565443} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565447}] :knowledge-point/title "Infinite Limits of Reciprocal Functions Where the Leading Term Has an Even Power"} :step/id #uuid "460493e8-2d85-52f9-b7dd-35205179f738" :step/math-academy-id 375}] :activity/title "Limits of Reciprocal Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124705}]}
